C = [
("h1", "11. Bought with a Price, and Died For"),
("p", "Claim: Paul does not keep the Cross for the passages of doctrine. He uses it in exhortation, in short sentences that stand at the end of an argument about conduct: “ye are bought with a price,” “for whom Christ died,” “Who died for us, that, whether we wake or sleep, we should live together with him.” The sentences do not explain the Cross. They assume it and press it on the reader. This chapter reads them as they stand and asks what they add to the passages already read."),
("sub", "11.1 “Ye are bought with a price” (1 Corinthians 6:19, 20)"),
("p", "“What? know ye not that your body is the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you, which ye have of God, and ye are not your own? For ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body, and in your spirit, which are God’s” (1 Corinthians 6:19, 20). The sentence stands at the end of Paul’s argument against fornication. The reason for the command is that the readers are not their own, and the ground of that is that they are bought with a price. The verse names the purchase and does not name the price. It does not say that the price was blood, or that it was paid to anyone. It says that a price was paid, and it draws the conclusion that the readers are to glorify God in the body."),
("p", "The volume does not rest on the Greek. The point is the use. The Cross is here a ground of ownership: the readers belong to God because of what was paid, and so the body is not theirs to use as they please."),
("sub", "11.2 “For whom Christ died” (1 Corinthians 8:11; Romans 14:15)"),
("p", "“And through thy knowledge shall the weak brother perish, for whom Christ died?” (1 Corinthians 8:11). “But if thy brother be grieved with thy meat, now walkest thou not charitably. Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died” (Romans 14:15). The two sentences have the same clause. In each, a brother is described as one for whom Christ died, and the description is used to show how serious it is to wound him. The clause does not say how many brothers or which. It is used of a fellow believer in the church, a person Paul can name as a brother."),
("ctbl", "What the two sentences say and what they leave unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“the weak brother perish, for whom Christ died”", "A weak brother may perish through another’s knowledge; Christ died for him", "What “perish” means here, and whether a brother for whom Christ died can perish in the sense of lost; the question is put, not answered"),
    ("“Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died”", "Do not destroy a brother by meat; Christ died for him", "The extent of the death beyond the brother named"),
    ("“ye sin against Christ” (1 Corinthians 8:12)", "To wound the weak conscience of the brethren is to sin against Christ", "The relation of the weak brother to Christ in more detail"),
  ], [0.30, 0.36, 0.34]),
("p", "The sentences belong to Paul’s teaching on liberty and conscience, which belong to another book. This volume notes only how the Cross is used in them. It is the measure of a brother’s worth. The brother is worth the death of Christ, and the argument turns on that."),
("sub", "11.3 “That he might be Lord both of the dead and living” (Romans 14:9)"),
("p", "“For to this end Christ both died, and rose, and revived, that he might be Lord both of the dead and living” (Romans 14:9). The death and resurrection are here given a purpose: that he might be Lord. The verse stands in the middle of the passage on judging a brother, and it says that the purpose of the death and rising is the lordship of Christ over the dead and the living. It adds to the purposes already read. The Cross was for sins (1 Corinthians 15:3), for the declaration of God’s righteousness (Romans 3:25), for the curse (Galatians 3:13), and, here, for the Lordship. The volume does not rank the purposes."),
("sub", "11.4 “Who died for us” (1 Thessalonians 5:9, 10)"),
("p", "“For God hath not appointed us to wrath, but to obtain salvation by our Lord Jesus Christ, Who died for us, that, whether we wake or sleep, we should live together with him” (1 Thessalonians 5:9, 10). The sentence joins the death of Christ to a hope. God has not appointed “us” to wrath. He has appointed us to obtain salvation by the Lord Jesus Christ, who died for us, and the purpose is that we should live together with him whether we wake or sleep. The volume reads the sentence as written. It does not say when the living together takes place, and it does not argue the order of events, which belongs to other books."),
("tbl", "The Cross in exhortation.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("1Cor 6:20", "“ye are bought with a price: therefore glorify God in your body”", "A price paid; therefore glorify God in the body"),
    ("1Cor 8:11", "“the weak brother perish, for whom Christ died”", "The brother is worth the death of Christ"),
    ("Rom 14:9", "“Christ both died, and rose, and revived, that he might be Lord”", "Purpose: lordship over the dead and living"),
    ("Rom 14:15", "“Destroy not him with thy meat, for whom Christ died”", "Do not destroy a brother"),
    ("1Thess 5:10", "“Who died for us, that, whether we wake or sleep, we should live together with him”", "Purpose: living together with him"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "11.5 What the chapter adds"),
("p", "Four things. The Cross is a ground of ownership, a measure of a brother’s worth, a purpose of lordship, and a ground of hope. None of these is among the problems of the earlier chapters, and none replaces them. They show that Paul can reach for the Cross in the middle of ordinary instruction, and that when he does he says only what the sentence needs. A volume that reduced the Cross to one purpose would have no place for these sentences. The volume that reads each passage as it stands has a place for them."),
]
